    <h2>Inside Chittorgarh Fort</h2>

    <h3 id="fort">1. Chittorgarh Fort</h3>
    <p>
        India&rsquo;s largest fort and a UNESCO World Heritage Site, spread over roughly 700 acres on a hilltop. Most of the
        city&rsquo;s famous monuments are inside its walls. Plan at least half a day. Our
        <a href="Chittorgarh-Fort-Travel-Guide.aspx">Chittorgarh Fort travel guide</a> covers history, timings and tickets in detail.
    </p>

    <h3 id="vijay-stambh">2. Vijay Stambh (Tower of Victory)</h3>
    <p>
        A nine-storey, roughly 37-metre-tall tower built by Maharana Kumbha in the 15th century. Every surface is carved with
        figures of gods, goddesses and scenes from Hindu epics.
    </p>

    <h3 id="kirti-stambh">3. Kirti Stambh (Tower of Fame)</h3>
    <p>
        A 12th-century Jain tower dedicated to Adinath. It is smaller than Vijay Stambh but equally beautiful, and usually far
        less crowded.
    </p>

    <h3 id="padmini-palace">4. Rani Padmini Palace</h3>
    <p>
        A white palace on the edge of a lotus pool with a small pavilion in the water, forever linked with the legend of
        Rani Padmini and Alauddin Khalji.
    </p>

    <h3 id="meera-temple">5. Meera Temple and Kumbha Shyam Temple</h3>
    <p>
        A peaceful temple complex associated with the saint-poet Meera Bai. The larger Kumbha Shyam Temple has a tall
        Indo-Aryan style spire and fine stone carvings.
    </p>

    <h3 id="kalika-mata">6. Kalika Mata Temple</h3>
    <p>
        Originally an 8th-century Sun temple, later dedicated to Goddess Kalika. It is especially lively during Navratri.
    </p>

    <h3 id="kumbha-palace">7. Rana Kumbha Palace and Light &amp; Sound Show</h3>
    <p>
        The atmospheric ruins of the oldest palace in the fort. In the evening, a light and sound show narrates the story of
        Chittorgarh &ndash; a great way to end your sightseeing day.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gaumukh">8. Gaumukh Kund</h3>
    <p>
        A sacred spring-fed reservoir where water flows from a cow-mouth shaped rock. The cliff-edge setting offers one of the
        best viewpoints in the fort.
    </p>

    <h3 id="fateh-prakash">9. Fateh Prakash Palace Museum</h3>
    <p>
        A palace built by Maharana Fateh Singh that now displays sculptures, arms, costumes and paintings from the Mewar region.
    </p>

    <h2>Around Chittorgarh (Day Trips)</h2>

    <h3 id="sanwariya">10. Sanwariya Seth Temple (about 40 km)</h3>
    <p>
        The famous temple of Shri Sanwaliya Seth (Lord Krishna) at Mandaphiya attracts lakhs of devotees every year. It is a
        short detour from the Chittorgarh&ndash;Udaipur highway. See our
        <a href="Sanwariya-Seth-Temple-Taxi-from-Chittorgarh.aspx">Sanwariya Seth darshan guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="bassi">11. Bassi Wildlife Sanctuary (about 25 km)</h3>
    <p>
        A forested sanctuary in the Vindhya hills, home to antelopes, wild boar, jackals and many birds. The nearby Bassi
        village is known for its traditional wooden handicrafts.
    </p>

    <h3 id="menal">12. Menal Waterfall and Temples (about 90 km)</h3>
    <p>
        A dramatic gorge with a waterfall that is at its best in the monsoon, along with the beautifully carved
        Mahanaleshwar temple complex. Menal is a scenic half-day or full-day trip.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p class="callout-title"><i class="fas fa-plus-circle text-brand"></i>Also worth a visit</p>
        <p>
            <strong>Nagari</strong> (ancient Madhyamika, one of the oldest settlements in Rajasthan), <strong>Matri Kundiya</strong>
            (a holy bathing site often called the &ldquo;Haridwar of Mewar&rdquo;) and <strong>Bijaipur</strong> with its
            heritage castle are all within easy reach by taxi.
        </p>
    </div>

